---
import ServiceLayout from '../../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import { A } from '../../lib/assets';

const showcase = [
  { asset: A.stoneFireplaceTall, caption: 'Stacked-stone fireplace with an arched firebox and raised hearth.' },
  { asset: A.poolFireplace, caption: 'Cast stone fireplace and chimney set beside the pool.' },
  { asset: A.brickFireplaceMantel, caption: 'Stone and brick fireplace with a timber mantel under a covered patio.' },
  { asset: A.firePitSeating, caption: 'Round fire pit with a curved stone seating wall, poolside.' },
  { asset: A.concreteCounter, caption: 'Outdoor kitchen counter with a stainless built-in.' },
  { asset: A.stoneVilla, caption: 'Stone columns carrying a covered outdoor living space.' },
];

const gallery = [
  A.pergolaLounge, A.patioSteps, A.poolWaterFeature,
  A.pergolaPatio, A.gazeboLawn, A.waterGarden,
];
---

<ServiceLayout
  title="Outdoor Kitchens & Fireplaces"
  eyebrow="Outdoor living"
  intro="Backyard kitchens, fireplaces, fire pits and counters — built in stone and brick to work with the pool, the patio and the planting that are already there."
  description="Outdoor kitchen Westlake Village and backyard fireplaces across Thousand Oaks, Calabasas and the Conejo Valley. Fire pits, seating walls, stone counters and BBQ surrounds, built by a licensed masonry contractor."
  headerAsset={A.pergolaLounge}
  serviceName="Outdoor kitchens, fireplaces and fire pits"
  showcase={showcase}
  gallery={gallery}
  galleryHeading="Patios, pools and outdoor living"
>
  <h2>Backyard kitchens across Westlake Village and the Conejo Valley</h2>
  <p>
    An outdoor kitchen is masonry with appliances in it. The structure — the
    block core, the counter substrate, the veneer, the drainage under the slab —
    is what decides whether it still looks right in ten years, and it is entirely
    invisible by the time the grill goes in.
  </p>
  <p>
    CPF builds backyard kitchens, fireplaces, fire pits, seating walls and
    counters as part of the wider hardscape, so the paving, the pool surround and
    the cooking end read as one piece of work rather than three projects that
    happened to land in the same garden.
  </p>

  <h3>What that covers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Outdoor kitchens and BBQ surrounds</strong> — block core, veneer, and counters cut to the appliances.</li>
    <li><strong>Fireplaces and chimneys</strong> — stacked stone, cast stone or brick, with proper flue detailing.</li>
    <li><strong>Fire pits and seating walls</strong> — sized so people can actually sit round them.</li>
    <li><strong>Counters</strong> — poured concrete or stone, sealed for outdoor use.</li>
    <li><strong>Patio and pergola bases</strong> — the flatwork and footings underneath.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Building around a pool</h3>
  <p>
    A lot of this work happens within a few feet of water, which changes things:
    setting out around existing coping, keeping the pool clean through a masonry
    build, and choosing finishes that survive chlorine and wet feet. It is worth
    saying out loud at the quoting stage rather than discovering on day three.
  </p>

  <h2>Guaranteed completion, fully insured</h2>
  <p>
    Nobody wants a half-built fireplace in the garden over summer. Every job
    carries a <strong>guaranteed completion date</strong> and <strong>full
    insurance coverage</strong>.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
